STEP 2 - optional, but it is what puts population and the map on the page
  serverSnapshot(ctx, which) is already imported into server.mjs, so one call per server
  asks the server itself. Build the ctx exactly the way the /server commands do (the same
  guild settings and the same rconRun), then, inside a try/catch so a dead server never
  breaks the page:

    const snap = await serverSnapshot(ctx, 'players');
    entry.players = typeof snap.players === 'number' ? snap.players : null;
    entry.maxPlayers = typeof snap.max === 'number' ? snap.max : null;

  and for the map name, the same with serverSnapshot(ctx, 'info'). Keep the 30 second
  cache above: the site's own proxy already caches for 60 seconds, so the bot is asked at
  most about once a minute no matter how many people open the page.

STEP 3 - one line that makes the status page's history start recording servers
  The site's proxy already looks for a 'servers' count in the bot's status stats
  (worker.js, proxyApi -> recordSample), it just never receives one. In the publicStatus
  object, change:

      stats: { guilds: state.guilds, gateway: state.gateway },

  to:

      stats: { guilds: state.guilds, gateway: state.gateway, servers: (settings.servers || []).length },

  From then on /api/history carries the server count next to uptime and guilds, and the
  status page can chart it without another change on the site.

AFTER APPLYING
  Push, and let Railway rebuild (it redeploys on push). Then open
  https://spinback-j7ao5f.camelai.app/servers.html - the meta line under the filter row
  says which source it read: "the bot's server list" once this route exists, or "the bot's
  public status" while it does not.